body:
Research notes for the implementer — these are notes to help, not a spec. Please still read the issue body and CLAUDE.md yourself and verify anything critical before making the change.

**Confirmed current state.** I read app/components/CueEditModal.tsx directly. Line 493 is exactly as the issue describes:

    key={`${existing ? `edit-${existing.id}` : "add"}-${autoOpenSection ?? "none"}`}

This is inside the JSX returned starting at the `return (` on line 395, itself inside the `CueEditModal` function component (starts line 94). The load-bearing WHY comment sits directly above it at lines 486-492 (re-mount so TimeStepper's autoOpen mount-only effect fires fresh when switching Add/Edit or between deep-linked fields).

**Important gotcha the issue's suggested diff glosses over: placement of the new `const` lines.** JSX children can't contain bare statements — you cannot literally insert `const idPart = ...` and `const timeStepperKey = ...` between the `<View ...>` open tag (line 484) and `<TimeStepper` (line 485), because that's inside the JSX tree, not a function body. The two `const`s need to go in the plain-JS part of the component body, before the `return (` on line 395. There's an existing precedent for this pattern in the same component: other derived values used inside the same JSX tree (`tz`, `cd`, `maxAlertMins`, `canSave`, `alertData`) are all computed as plain `const`s between the `if (!visible) return null;` guard (line 229) and `return (` (line 395), then referenced by name inside the JSX. The cleanest spot is right before `return (` at line 395, next to where `canSave`/`alertData` etc. already live — that keeps `existing` and `autoOpenSection` (both in scope as props throughout the whole function body) available and matches the file's existing style.

**Fix shape**, adapted for that placement:

    const idPart = existing ? `edit-${existing.id}` : "add";
    const timeStepperKey = `${idPart}-${autoOpenSection ?? "none"}`;

    return (
      ...
      <TimeStepper
        // (existing WHY comment, unchanged or moved up here)
        key={timeStepperKey}
        ...

Moving the WHY comment (lines 486-492) to sit above the new `const timeStepperKey` line (or leaving a short pointer comment in both places) satisfies CLAUDE.md's comment rule (short, explains non-obvious WHY) — don't just delete it.

**Byte-for-byte string equivalence.** The produced string must be identical to today's output for every state combination (existing present/absent × autoOpenSection one of "time"/"zone"/"alert"/"name"/undefined), since this key is load-bearing for the remount/autoOpen behavior documented in CLAUDE.md's Architecture section. The suggested two-line extraction is a pure syntactic transform (same ternary, same nullish-coalescing, same interpolation order) so this holds, but it's worth a quick sanity check after editing — e.g. confirm `existing.id`'s type (a string per the `Props`/cue type in this file) is used the same way.

**Scope check.** I grepped and found no other reference to this exact template-literal expression elsewhere in the codebase — this is a self-contained, single-file change limited to CueEditModal.tsx.

**On the Sonar rule itself.** typescript:S4624 ("Template literals should not be nested") is a real SonarSource code-smell rule; its documented fix is exactly what the issue proposes — pull the inner template literal into its own named variable so the outer template only interpolates plain values. (Reference: rules.sonarsource.com/typescript/rspec-4624 and the eslint-plugin-sonarjs docs for the same rule.) Note this repo's own app/eslint.config.js (Expo's flat config) does not include this Sonar rule locally, so `npm run lint` inside app/ won't catch it either way — that's expected and not something to "fix" as part of this change.

**No CLAUDE.md conflicts found.** This is a pure refactor with no behavior, license, sync, offline, or timer-visibility implications.

What's worth improving
Inside the alert-minutes picker's row renderer, the `label` prop is built with a ternary whose false-branch template literal contains a second ternary for pluralizing "minute"/"minutes". Nested ternaries like this are harder to scan at a glance than they look, because the reader has to notice the inner conditional is a separate decision (singular vs plural) rather than part of the outer one (none vs some).

Where
app/components/CueEditModal.tsx:273, inside `renderAlertRow` (the `FlatList` row renderer used by the alert-minutes picker):

```tsx
const renderAlertRow: ListRenderItem<number> = ({ item, index }) => {
  const isNone = item === 0;
  return (
    <AlertOptionRow
      label={isNone ? "None" : `${item} minute${item === 1 ? "" : "s"} before`}
      ...
```

Sonar rule and message
typescript:S3358 — "Extract this nested ternary operation into an independent statement."

Suggested fix
Pull the inner (plural-suffix) ternary out into its own `const` before the `return`, so each conditional decision reads as one line instead of being nested inside another expression:

```tsx
const renderAlertRow: ListRenderItem<number> = ({ item, index }) => {
  const isNone = item === 0;
  const suffix = item === 1 ? "" : "s";
  return (
    <AlertOptionRow
      label={isNone ? "None" : `${item} minute${suffix} before`}
      ...
```

Smallest safe way to do this
This is a pure local-variable extraction inside a single row renderer — no change to `form.alertMinutesBefore`, `onPress`, or any state/prop shape, and no change to what text ends up on screen for any value of `item`. It only touches this one render function, so the blast radius is a single JSX attribute in one list row component.

CLAUDE.md invariants to respect
This is in `app/`, the core product, so keep the change purely cosmetic/structural: don't touch the countdown or alert-scheduling logic elsewhere in this file, don't add any comment beyond something a non-technical reader could follow (or skip a comment entirely, since the extracted `suffix` line is self-explanatory), and avoid introducing any new semantic line breaks.
